<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gwanggaeto Stele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">414</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Stele of Gwanggaeto the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gwanggaeto Stele is a granite monument about six metres tall erected in 414 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangsu-of-goguryeo">King Jangsu</a> of Goguryeo at the old capital, present-day Ji'an in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in honour of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great">Gwanggaeto the Great</a>. Its inscription of some 1,800 Chinese characters records Goguryeo's founding legend, Gwanggaeto's campaigns, including an expedition of 400 to help Silla against the Wa, and lists of tomb-guard households.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele.jpg" alt="Gwanggaeto Stele" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yumeto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20230605_Stela_for_Gwanggaeto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Carved from a single mass of granite with a girth of almost four metres, the stele stands near Gwanggaeto's tomb at Ji'an on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a>. Its Classical Chinese text, covering all four sides, falls into three parts. The first tells how the founder, King Ch'umo, came from Northern Buyeo as son of the Celestial Emperor and the daughter of Habaek, crossed a river on floating turtles and founded his capital, the throne passing down to the seventeenth king, Gwanggaeto.</p><p>The second part lists Gwanggaeto's campaigns from the conquest of the Paeryeo in 395, among them the surrender of the Baekje king in 396, who gave captives and a thousand bolts of cloth, and the defeat of Wa troops on the southern border in 404, followed by the conquest of Eastern Buyeo in 410. Its seven conquests agree with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a>. The third part names the Sumyoin, the households appointed to guard the king's tomb.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>After the fall of Goguryeo in 668 the region lay outside Korean and Chinese politics, and the Qing closed it to settlement. When settlers arrived in the 19th century, a local official, Guan Yueshan, found the stele around 1876 under centuries of mud and growth. In 1882 the county magistrate had the vegetation burnt off, damaging the surface. In 1883 the Japanese officer Sakō Kageaki, travelling disguised as a herbalist, took a tracing of the text home, and the Japanese General Staff had scholars decode it, publishing their findings in 1889, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> knew of the monument.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The stele has become a focus of national rivalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, above all over the sinmyo passage for the year 391. Japanese scholars traditionally read it as saying that the Wa crossed the sea and made Baekje and Silla their subjects; Korean scholars, beginning with Chŏng In-bo in 1955, read Goguryeo as the subject. In 1972 Lee Jin-hui claimed the Japanese army had altered the stele with lime, but Chinese scholars such as Wang Jianqun and Xu Jianxin, who found a rubbing made before 1881, concluded there was no evidence of deliberate damage. An exact replica stands at the War Memorial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gwanggaeto Stele” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Gwanggaeto Stele</b>, rediscovered around 1880, is among the most important and most contested inscriptions in East Asian history, especially for its passages on the Wa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Pressed by Wa raiders from Japan and by Gaya, Naemul sought help from Goguryeo, and according to the <b>Gwanggaeto Stele</b> an army of that king came south in 400 to relieve Silla, which remained under Goguryeo influence for much of the fifth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
